{
  "units": [
    {
      "title": "Uncovered Interest Parity (UIP) Components",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0021", "FCT_0066", "FCT_0067", "FCT_0068", "FCT_0069", "FCT_0070"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0021", "FCT_0066", "FCT_0067", "FCT_0068", "FCT_0069", "FCT_0070"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0021",
              "exact_quote": "The uncovered interest parity is the extra equation needed when an endogenous variable such as a floating exchange rate is introduced."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0066",
              "exact_quote": "The exchange rate is floating."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0067",
              "exact_quote": "And in the denominator, you have the foreign interest rate."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0068",
              "exact_quote": "Well, remember, I can divide this by the exchange rate on both sides."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0069",
              "exact_quote": "And then what you get is 1 equal to a numerator that has the nominal exchange rate times the expected appreciation of the currency plus."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0070",
              "exact_quote": "And so when you compare the two, you have to compare one base interest rate, the domestic of the foreign, plus expected appreciation of the appreciation of that currency."
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
            "type": "concept_tree",
            "title": "UIP Equation Elements",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "n0",
                "label": "Uncovered Interest Parity (UIP)",
                "description": "extra equation for floating exchange rate"
              },
              {
                "id": "n1",
                "label": "Floating exchange rate",
                "parent": "n0",
                "description": "exchange rate is floating"
              },
              {
                "id": "n2",
                "label": "Domestic interest rate",
                "parent": "n0"
              },
              {
                "id": "n3",
                "label": "Foreign interest rate",
                "parent": "n0",
                "description": "in denominator"
              },
              {
                "id": "n4",
                "label": "Nominal exchange rate",
                "parent": "n0",
                "description": "appears in numerator"
              },
              {
                "id": "n5",
                "label": "Expected appreciation of currency",
                "parent": "n0",
                "description": "multiplied with nominal exchange rate"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest Rate Differential and Expected Depreciation",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0025", "CON_0026", "EXP_0003", "EXP_0004"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0025", "CON_0026", "EXP_0003", "EXP_0004"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0025",
              "exact_quote": "When one bond pays a higher interest rate than another, the higher return must be offset by an expected depreciation of the currency of the higher‑interest bond."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0026",
              "exact_quote": "The anticipated fall in the value of a currency that is used to offset a higher interest rate on a bond denominated in that currency."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "EXP_0003",
              "exact_quote": "So what I need to do is compensate say, for the interest rate differential with an expected depreciation of the currency that's paying a higher interest rate."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "EXP_0004",
              "exact_quote": "When the currency appreciates enough, the higher‑interest advantage disappears because the expected depreciation component is reduced."
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
            "type": "flowchart",
            "title": "Mechanism of Interest Rate Differential",
            "direction": "DOWN",
            "nodes": [
              { "id": "s", "label": "Higher domestic interest rate", "kind": "start" },
              { "id": "a1", "label": "Higher return on bond", "kind": "action" },
              { "id": "d1", "label": "Need offset", "kind": "decision" },
              { "id": "a2", "label": "Expected depreciation of currency", "kind": "action" },
              { "id": "s2", "label": "Currency appreciation", "kind": "decision" },
              { "id": "e1", "label": "Depreciation reduced → advantage lost", "kind": "success" }
            ],
            "edges": [
              { "from": "s", "to": "a1", "label": "" },
              { "from": "a1", "to": "d1", "label": "" },
              { "from": "d1", "to": "a2", "label": "yes" },
              { "from": "a2", "to": "s2", "label": "" },
              { "from": "s2", "to": "e1", "label": "appreciates enough" }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Consumer Confidence and Aggregate Demand",
      "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0020"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["CON_0020"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0020",
              "exact_quote": "Remember, if the consumer confidence goes up, then aggregate demand goes up."
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
            "type": "mind_map",
            "title": "Consumer Confidence Effect",
            "nodes": [
              { "id": "root", "label": "Consumer Confidence" },
              { "id": "c1", "label": "Increases", "parent": "root" },
              { "id": "c2", "label": "Aggregate Demand", "parent": "c1", "description": "goes up" }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fixed Price Assumption",
      "source_segment_ids": ["FCT_0065", "CON_0019"],
      "mode": "visual_only",
      "remaining_text": null,
      "visuals": [
        {
          "source_segment_ids": ["FCT_0065", "CON_0019"],
          "needs_review": false,
          "missing_information": [],
          "evidence": [
            {
              "note_id": "FCT_0065",
              "exact_quote": "Both the domestic and foreign prices are assumed to be completely fixed."
            },
            {
              "note_id": "CON_0019",
              "exact_quote": "Real exchange rate moves only with nominal exchange rate when prices are fixed"
            }
          ],
          "payload": {
            "type": "concept_tree",
            "title": "Fixed Prices and Exchange Rate Relationship",
            "nodes": [
              { "id": "n0", "label": "Fixed Prices", "description": "domestic and foreign prices fixed" },
              { "id": "n1", "label": "Nominal Exchange Rate", "parent": "n0", "description": "only variable moving" },
              { "id": "n2", "label": "Real Exchange Rate", "parent": "n0", "description": "moves with nominal rate" }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
